# Frontend Design System

## Tokens only

- Use semantic token utilities (`bg-card`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, `bg-danger-soft`, ...) -- never a raw palette class (`bg-slate-800`, `text-emerald-300`, ...) or a hex value, in any new or changed file.
- Check before finishing a change:
  `rg "(slate|gray|zinc|neutral|stone|red|green|emerald|amber|yellow|orange|blue|sky|indigo|violet|purple|pink|rose)-[0-9]{2,3}" frontend/src`
  must return nothing for every file you touched.
- Token roles and their values live in `frontend/src/index.css` (`:root, [data-theme='dark']` is dark -- the default absent a stored preference or an OS "light" signal; `[data-theme='light']` is light).
- Radius and most of the type scale intentionally reuse Tailwind's own defaults rather than a custom set -- see the comment at the top of `index.css` for the mapping (e.g. `rounded-lg` is the design's "md" radius).
- **Theme.** A System/Light/Dark toggle (`ThemeMenu` in the TopBar) backed by `ThemeProvider` / `useTheme` (`src/utils/useTheme.ts`); the choice persists in `localStorage` (`evalsvc.theme.v1`). `index.html` sets `data-theme` from that same storage key (or the OS setting, for "System") in an inline pre-paint script, before the bundle loads -- keep that script in sync by hand with `utils/theme.ts`'s `resolveTheme()` if the resolution logic ever changes.

## Primitives first

- Compose from `src/components/` (`Button`, `Card`, `Dialog`, `TextInput`, `Badge`, ...) instead of hand-styling a one-off `<button>`, bordered `<div>`, or native `<select>`.
- If a primitive is missing, add a small one to `src/components/` rather than stretching an existing primitive to fit or styling inline.
- Radix UI backs the overlay/structural primitives (`Dialog`, `Popover`, `Tooltip`, `Menu`, `Select`, `SegmentedControl`, `MultiSelectMenu`); form fields (`TextInput`, `Checkbox`, `Radio`) are styled native elements, not custom listboxes. `SelectField` is built on Radix `Select`, not a styled native `<select>` -- its popover opens the same in-app menu `Menu`, `MultiSelectMenu` and `ThemeMenu` do, and its trigger shares one `selectTriggerClassName` helper (`SelectField.helper.ts`) with `MultiSelectMenu`'s own, so every dropdown trigger in the app is one shape regardless of which popover opens underneath it. `SearchInput` and `SegmentedControl` both require an `aria-label` prop (TypeScript-enforced, not just a convention) -- neither has a visible `<label>` anywhere it's used.
- Every toolbar control -- `SelectField`, `MultiSelectMenu`'s trigger, `SegmentedControl`, `FilterChip`, a labelled `Button` -- stands at the same `h-9` (36px), so a filter row never mixes control heights.
- `FilterChip` is one active, removable filter pill (`label`, `value`, `onClear`, `clearLabel`) -- `RunsToolbar`'s own Batch chip and `SampleFilters`' own Rule pill both build on it instead of hand-rolling their own bordered span.
- `HoverCard` is a Popover variant that opens on hover *or* focus and renders with no `Portal`, so its content stays in the natural tab order right after its trigger -- reach for it instead of Radix's own `react-hover-card` (not installed; its content is deliberately outside the tab order). `IntervalWhisker` is a plain-SVG confidence-interval whisker for a shared axis -- no chart library for a shape this simple; it defaults to a 96px row-height width, or pass `width` for a wider one (the compare page's forest plot draws several at 240px on one shared axis). `CopyLinkButton` is `CopyButton`'s labelled sibling for "copy the current URL".
- `TabNav` is a `NavLink` strip for path-based tabs -- a detail page's own nested routes (e.g. a run report's Overview/Samples/Configuration/Logs), each one its own route rather than local component state.
- `Dialog` takes an optional `size` (`'md'` default, `'lg'`, `'xl'` -- the wider two are for Compare's Add run picker and its wide side-by-side sample view). Each size is one complete width class (`Dialog.helper.ts`); never add a width utility of your own on top of it.
- `SidePanel` is Dialog's right-docked sibling, built on the same Radix Dialog primitive: title, optional description, a scrolling body and an optional sticky footer, for a form or detail view wide enough to want its own drawer (New evaluation's own "Customize" panels; a future catalog "Manage" drawer). Reach for `Dialog` instead when the content is short enough to read as a centred modal.
- `Stepper` is a horizontal step indicator for a flow whose steps live in page state (New evaluation's own Choose/Settings/Review, one component's `useState`, not three routes). Reach for `TabNav` instead once each step is its own route.
- `JsonDetails` is a collapsible, copyable "raw JSON, verbatim" view -- `Disclosure` + `CodeBlock`, shared by `InspectionSummary`'s config.json disclosure and a model's own Configuration tab (`generation_config`) instead of two hand-rolled `<details><pre>` pairs.
- `Disclosure` is the shared `<details>`/`<summary>` shell underneath `JsonDetails` -- the native marker hidden, a `ChevronRight` that rotates 90° open, and two sizes (`md` default; `sm` for a quieter toggle, `JsonDetails`' own size and `CatalogPanel`'s state legend). A button-driven expand/collapse that can't be a real `<details>` (`RunsBatchHeaderRow`'s batch row, the sample Breakdown panel) still follows the same chevron-before-label, rotate-90 convention by hand, so every expand/collapse in the app reads as one gesture.
- `CodeBlock` is the `<pre>` + `CopyButton` shell `JsonDetails` composes, for plain text rather than JSON -- a benchmark's own prompt template or source YAML. Reach for `JsonDetails` instead when the value is an object to stringify; reach for `CodeBlock` directly when it's already text.
- `Callout` is the one tinted-box-with-icon primitive: a required `tone` (`'info' | 'warning' | 'danger'`), an optional `title`, and an optional `actions` slot. `InspectionSummary.tsx` and `DryRunPreview.tsx` both build on it rather than their own hand-rolled `bg-warning-soft`/`bg-danger-soft` boxes; the Catalog health banner was its first caller.
- Compose class names with `cn()` (`src/utils/cn.ts`). There is no `tailwind-merge` in this project: never let two utilities that touch the same CSS property (e.g. `px-3` and `pl-9`) land in the same class string -- see `Button.helper.ts` / `TextInput.helper.ts` for the pattern of giving each caller one complete, non-conflicting size/padding string instead.
- `/styleguide` (dev only, not part of the real app) shows every primitive in every state, in both themes.

## State patterns (§4.5 of the redesign plan)

| Pattern | Rule |
|---|---|
| Loading | `Skeleton` shaped like the final layout (`motion-safe:animate-pulse`). Never the text "Loading…". A page-level `*Skeleton` root also gets `aria-busy` plus screen-reader-only loading text. |
| Empty | `EmptyState`: icon (defaults to `Inbox`), title, one sentence, one action. |
| Error | `ErrorState`: plain message, Retry, a Details disclosure for the raw error. |
| Feedback | `Toaster` for mutation success/failure; inline text for form validation. |
| Destructive or costly action | `ConfirmDialog` stating exactly what happens. Never `window.confirm`. |
| Numbers | `tabular-nums` on numeric table columns; percent with one decimal; margin of error as `±x.x`. |

Any decorative animation -- a pulse, a spin, a rotate transition -- is prefixed `motion-safe:`, so `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` suppresses it. `Skeleton`'s pulse, `Spinner`'s spin, `RunStatusChip`/`ModelServerCard`'s status-icon spin, `LiveIndicator`'s pulse dot, and the sample breakdown's chevron rotate all follow this.

## Vocabulary

UI copy is plain language -- no doc filenames, decision ids, or internal API paths. The full mapping is §4.3 of `docs/UI_REDESIGN_PLAN.md`; a few load-bearing ones:

- `checkpoint` -> **Model** (the word "checkpoint" stays only in registration and technical fields).
- `standard` -> **Benchmark**.
- `comparison_hash` -> **Setup** (never show the raw hash as the primary label).
- `eval_run` -> **Run**; `run_group` -> **Batch**.

A term that can't be translated away entirely (`fingerprint`, `samples`, `truncated`, `batch`, `weights`, `samplingProfile`, `servingProfile`, `superseded`, ...) gets a one-sentence definition in `TERM_HINTS` (`src/utils/labels.ts`) and a `TermLabel` wrapper on its header or field label -- a dotted underline plus a focusable `Tooltip`, not a raw term left unexplained. Add to `TERM_HINTS` rather than inventing a tooltip string at the call site.

## Data and domain display

- Fetch through a hook in `src/api/queries/` (`useLeaderboard`, `useRuns`, `useCheckpoint`, ...) -- never write an inline `queryKey: [...]` array at a call site. Every key lives in `src/api/queries/queryKeys.ts`; a mutation invalidates by importing the same `queryKeys.*()` call it wants to invalidate, not by retyping the key.
- Show a model, benchmark, setup, score, fingerprint, timestamp or run status through its domain component (`ModelName`, `BenchmarkName`, `SetupChip`, `ScoreValue`, `FingerprintChip`, `RelativeTime`, `RunStatusChip` -- `src/components/`), not a raw string or a hand-rolled equivalent. Add a domain component for a new concept rather than formatting it inline at each call site.
- Score and margin-of-error formatting goes through `src/utils/formatScore.ts` (`formatScore`, `formatScoreWithUnit`, `formatMargin`); duration and relative time through `formatDuration.ts` and `formatRelativeTime.ts`. A metric's own `display` hint (`display_kind`, `display_multiplier`, `display_unit`, `display_precision`) always takes priority over the percent-with-one-decimal default.
- UI wording for a run's status, a checkpoint's weights, or a catalog entry's sync state comes from `src/utils/labels.ts`, not a string re-typed at the call site.
- A checkpoint's family label and grouping always go through `utils/familyGroups.ts` (`groupCheckpointsByFamily`, `familyKey`), never the raw `family` string directly. Spellings on file vary (`"QWen3.5"` vs `"Qwen-3.5"`); the label is the group's most common spelling, ties broken by the most recently registered model's spelling, so the Models list, the registration family input, the New-evaluation picker and the Leaderboard filter can never disagree on what a family is called.
